course: dictionary
lesson: 3630
title: Finery and fancy words
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Finery means fancy, expensive or very smart clothes and jewellery worn for a special occasion. Context helps you find it: «in all their finery» tells you people are dressed up, perhaps for a party, a wedding or a ball.
explain: Finery can also mean bright, showy decoration. The author liked the countryside «undecorated, hard, and stripped of its finery», so finery must be the decoration that has been taken away, leaving things plain.
explain: Finery can feel a little mocking when someone shows off. Ask yourself: what word would fit here? Then swap in your meaning, such as "in their best clothes", and check that the sentence still makes sense.
fact: Finery means fancy clothes and jewellery worn for a special occasion, or bright, showy decoration.
fact: «In all their finery» means dressed up in their very best clothes.
fact: «Stripped of its finery» means with all the decoration taken away, so it looks plain.
fact: Finery is an uncountable noun, so you say some finery, not a finery.
fact: In The Wind in the Willows, Toad loves dressing up, and in old tales the peacock shows off its finery.
exercise: «The guests arrived in all their finery.» What does finery mean here? | *Fancy, smart clothes for a special occasion | Rain and wind | Tiny pieces of paper | Muddy boots and old coats
exercise: «The countryside was stripped of its finery.» What does this suggest? | Full of noisy parties | *Plain, with the decoration taken away | Very hot and dry | Covered in bright new decorations
exercise: The peacock spread his bright ___ to show off to the other birds. Which word fits best? | *finery | fossil | ferry | fog
test: «She wore her finest finery to the ball.» What is finery? | *Fancy clothes and jewellery for a special occasion | Hard, bare stone | Bright, falling rain | A kind of fish
test: Why might an author describe someone's finery in a mocking way? | *Because the person is showing off too much | Because the finery is broken and torn | Because the clothes are very cheap | Because it is raining and cold
exam: Which word is closest in meaning to finery? | Silence | Dirt | *Decoration | Hunger
